taking all of these factors into  consideration  there are four points of advice i want  to highlight  the first is to reinforce that adults  18 years and above should be vaccinated  and we strongly advise that they are  vaccinated  the benefits outweigh the potential  harms  i am a chess physician at the hospital  that i work at  we are now seeing young people who are  unvaccinated being admitted to hospital  with quite severe covet many of them  need  oxygen support and sadly some of them  also need a ventilator machine to help  them to breathe  much of that suffering can be prevented  or reduced through vaccination and so my  urge  to add out to our 18 years and above who  are not yet vaccinated  is that you strongly consider booking  your vaccine appointment  having the job and getting yourselves  and your friends and family protected  that's the first piece of advice the  second relates to healthy  16 and 17 year olds  we would like to advise that the first  vaccine dose is offered  now for 16 and 17 year olds who are  otherwise healthy  with the aim of a second vaccine dose to  be offered  sometime later and we will provide  another update of advice  regarding the exact details of the  second dose  the reason for wanting to provide advice  on the second dose separately  is because we're expecting more data to  accumulate in the coming weeks  and that means that we can offer advice  that is the most up-to-date  and the most relevant for the second  dose for this particular age group  i like to reflect that you may remember  in the uk  at the end of december last year  we chose to use a two dose schedule with  an extended  dose interval between the first and  second dose  by doing so that allowed us to  prioritize first doses  and have more people protected we also  know now that  by extending the interval between the  doses we achieve  a higher level of protection which can  translate into a longer duration of  protection  with regard to the current advice the  extended dose interval means that we are  in a position to offer advice on the  first dose  and still have time to look at new data  as they accumulate  before second doses are due  other countries that have chosen to use  a three-week interval between their  doses  will not have this option quite so  readily available  to them and that is maybe an important  difference  this advice for healthy 16 and 17 year  olds is new  and is in addition to their existing  advice  for 16 and 17 year olds who have  underlying health conditions that put  them at risk of severe covet  they have been eligible for vaccination  for many months now  and i hope most of them have been  vaccinated  the third point of advice is for  children  aged 12 to 15 years old we offered  advice for the last month in july  and that advice is unchanged at this  time  just to summarize there are two groups  of people  in the age bracket 12 to 15 years  who are revising vaccination for  these are people with specific  underlying health conditions that  put them at risk of severe covid and  also  12 to 15 year olds who are close  household contacts  of a person who is immunosuppressed and  this is because  immunosuppressed persons may not be able  to generate a good immune response even  if they themselves are vaccinated  as i said this advice is unchanged from  july  the last and fourth piece of advice that  i like to  put forward today is i like to highlight  the importance  of other immunization programs that are  routinely offered  to children and young people such as  meningitis  human papilloma vaccines and the  influenza  vaccine as we increasingly return to  life as normal  we need to catch up on these other  vaccine programs as well  and so i would say that if you are  eligible for one of these vaccines  and are offered these vaccines please do  not ignore them  just because we're in a pandemic they  are important  and they are safe thank you
 

